sections.content:
Sanders priznaje da bi većina ljudi instinktivno rekla da točno zna o čemu otac Michael govori – da te stvari **doista ne razumiju**. Ali odmah nudi protuargument.

Klasični odgovor glasi: modeli samo predviđaju sljedeći token, riječ je samo o statističkim vjerojatnostima. Međutim, Anthropic je objavio istraživanje – voditelj priznaje da mu naziv rada ne pada na pamet – koje sugerira da postoji neka vrsta **razumijevanja točke do koje model želi doći** prije nego što uopće počne mapirati kamo ide u jeziku. Drugim riječima: postoji nešto poput **planiranja**.

Sanders zatim, za publiku, izlaže poznatu podjelu inteligencije na četiri sastavna područja koju koristi jedan od najpoznatijih istraživača u polju (u razgovoru spomenut kao Jan Kuhn):

1. **sposobnost razmišljanja (rezoniranja)**;
2. **sposobnost planiranja** slijeda djelovanja;
3. **trajna memorija**;
4. **model svijeta**.

Taj je istraživač poznat po tvrdnji da veliki jezični modeli nemaju ništa od toga i da stoga nisu doista inteligentni. Ali, kaže Sanders, Anthropic sada kao da govori "pričekajte trenutak" – jer se čini da modeli, kad im se postavi pitanje, imaju nekakvu naznaku odgovora koji žele dati prije nego što počnu s predviđanjem sljedećeg tokena.

Ako modeli počnu pokazivati da se u tom procesu događa **više nego što se mislilo**, zaključuje voditelj, to nam daje razlog da se zapitamo: je li papa u pravu? Ili možda razumiju mnogo više nego što mislimo – i ako je tako, što to znači?

sections.content_en:
Sanders admits that most people would instinctively say that they know exactly what Father Michael is talking about – that these things **really do not understand**. But he immediately offers a counterargument.

The classic answer is: models merely predict the next token, it is only a matter of statistical probabilities. However, Anthropic published research – the host admits that the title of the paper does not come to mind – which suggests that there is some kind of **understanding of the point the model wants to reach** before it even begins to map out where it is going in language. In other words: there is something like **planning**.

Sanders then, for the audience, outlines the well-known division of intelligence into four component areas used by one of the most famous researchers in the field (mentioned in the conversation as Jan Kuhn):

1. **the ability to think (reason)**;
2. **the ability to plan** a sequence of actions;
3. **persistent memory**;
4. **a world model**.

That researcher is known for claiming that large language models have none of that and are therefore not truly intelligent. But, Sanders says, Anthropic now seems to be saying "wait a moment" – because it seems that models, when asked a question, have some kind of indication of the answer they want to give before they begin predicting the next token.

If models begin to show that **more is happening in that process than was thought**, the host concludes, that gives us reason to ask ourselves: is the Pope right? Or do they perhaps understand much more than we think – and if so, what does that mean?
